{"items":[{"path":"tools.argent.describe","description":"Get the accessibility / DOM element tree for the current screen.
Use when you need element frames or labels and have no fresh tree: most interaction tools (not tv-remote or shake) already return one after each action, or name an identical earlier one by its tree id.
On iOS it reads the AXRuntime accessibility service, so it sees whatever is visible, including system dialogs and permission prompts. While a system dialog is visible it returns the dialog's elements (buttons, text); otherwise the foreground app's. On a physical iOS device it covers only the app started with launch-app; to read system dialogs there, launch-app `com.apple.springboard` first.
On Android it reads the tree through Argent's UiAutomation helper, falling back to `uiautomator dump`. On Chromium it walks the DOM over the Chrome DevTools Protocol: every visible element with its ARIA role, accessible name and bounding rect.
On Vega (Fire TV) it reads the on-device automation toolkit; `[focused]`/`[selected]` mark the D-pad cursor, which you move with `tv-remote` (Vega has no touch).
On a Mac app it reads the app's accessibility tree with web view content and React Native testIDs merged in; frames are fractions of the app window, grown to include sheets and menus past it.
On a TV target (Apple TV / Android TV: a `list-devices` entry with runtimeKind 'tv') it returns the FOCUSED element and the FOCUSABLE elements instead, since a TV UI has no tap coordinates. Move the highlight with `tv-remote`, then call describe again to confirm where focus landed.

Returns `{ description, source }`: `description` renders the tree one line per element, with its role, label/value/id, VoiceOver hint (iOS simulator), interactivity flags and frame. Frames are normalized [0,1] fractions of the screen / window width and height (not pixels); tap an element at the centre of its frame. The tree has no z-order or occlusion information: an element may be covered by an overlay (e.g. a toolbar over list rows), so check a screenshot when a tap lands unexpectedly.
Fails if the tree cannot be read, e.g. on a physical iOS device before launch-app has started an app.

For UIKit properties of a view (class name, accessibilityIdentifier), use native-find-views (iOS simulator only).
For React Native component names, or a testID describe does not list, use debugger-component-tree.","signature":"tools.argent.describe({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * App bundle ID, used as a target hint on iOS when the AX-service returns no elements and describe falls back to native-devtools inspection. If omitted, the fallback auto-detects the frontmost connected app. Ignored on every other platform, including a physical iOS device.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["await-screen-idle"]","description":"Block until the screen has rendered content and stopped changing, or the timeout elapses. It polls the same element tree as `describe`.
Use when a launch or navigation must finish rendering before a screenshot or tap.
Returns { settled, waitedMs, polls, note? }. If the screen never goes still before timeoutMs, it returns settled=false instead of failing.","signature":"tools.argent["await-screen-idle"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). @minLength 1 */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Max time to wait for the screen to settle before giving up (default 3000). Maximum: 120000. Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @maximum 120000 @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  timeoutMs?: number,
  /** How often to re-read the tree (default 200). Range: 50 to 5000. @integer @minimum 50 @maximum 5000 */
  pollIntervalMs?: number,
  /**
   * The screen must hold the same content for at least this long to count as settled (default 250). Range: 0 to 10000.
   * @integer @minimum 0 @maximum 10000
   */
  minStableMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["await-ui-element"]","description":"Block until a UI element reaches an expected state or the timeout elapses, so you don't have to poll screenshot/describe yourself. It polls the same tree as `describe`.
Use when the next step needs an element that appears or disappears asynchronously, e.g. after a tap or navigation, or before tapping something that loads late.
Returns { success: boolean, elapsed: number, note?, cause? }. If the condition does not hold by the timeout, it returns success=false, and that is not always a verdict on the condition: `cause` says which it was: `unmet` (the tree was read and the condition was false there), `unreadable` (no trustworthy read, so nothing was judged) or `cancelled`. `note` describes what was seen. Only `unmet` licenses rewriting the check.","signature":"tools.argent["await-ui-element"]({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * What to wait for. `exists`: the selector matches an element anywhere in the tree. `visible`: it matches one with a non-zero on-screen frame. `hidden`: it matches nothing, or only a zero-area element (e.g. a spinner that went away). `text`: the first visible match in reading order (topmost, then leftmost; the first match overall if none is visible) contains expectedText, or equals it with textMatch `equals`. Only that match is checked, so if a loose selector hits several elements, narrow it to the one holding the text. On a Mac app, a match in an open menu comes first, then one in an alert, sheet or popover, then one in the window.
   */
  condition: "exists" | "visible" | "hidden" | "text",
  /** Element to match. Give at least one field; every given field must match. */
  selector: {
    /** Case-insensitive substring of the element's visible label or value. @minLength 1 */
    text?: string,
    /**
     * The element's identifier (accessibilityIdentifier / resource-id / testid), matched case-insensitively as the exact identifier or the unqualified resource-id name ('submit' matches 'com.example.app:id/submit').
     * @minLength 1
     */
    identifier?: string,
    /** Case-insensitive substring of the element's role (e.g. AXButton, button, TextView). @minLength 1 */
    role?: string,
  },
  /**
   * Required for condition `text`: the string the checked match must contain or equal (see `textMatch`).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  expectedText?: string,
  /**
   * For condition `text`: how expectedText is compared. `contains` (default) is a case-insensitive substring; `equals` is a case-insensitive full-string match.
   */
  textMatch?: "contains" | "equals",
  /**
   * iOS app bundle id, passed to the describe fallback (see `describe`). Ignored on every other platform, including physical iOS.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
  /**
   * Max time to wait for the condition before giving up (default 5000). Maximum: 120000. Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @maximum 120000 @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  timeoutMs?: number,
  /** How often to re-check the tree (default 400). Range: 50 to 5000. @integer @minimum 50 @maximum 5000 */
  pollIntervalMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.fold","description":"Move the hinge of a foldable iOS simulator (the iPhone Duo) or Android emulator (a Pixel Fold or generic foldable AVD) to a `posture` or to an `angle`; pass exactly one of the two. The tool returns when the device accepts input again, so the next tap lands.
Use when you must see or test an app in a different posture. `list-devices` marks foldable devices with `foldable: true`. To turn a device without a fold, use `rotate`.
All tools use the active panel (the panel that shows the UI), also after a fold made outside argent. The coordinates and the screenshot size change with the panel. Before you tap, read the element tree in this result or run `describe` again.
iOS: unfolded, the UI is landscape, as on a rotated iPhone. A fold between two angles that are not closed or open can keep the current panel. To change panels, fold to closed or open.
iOS returns { activeScreen, screen: { id, panel, width, height }, posture?, hingeAngle, warning? }. screen names the active panel and gives its size in pixels. posture is set only at 0°, 120° and 180°. warning is set when argent cannot find the active panel, or when the device did not change to the expected panel.
Android returns { posture?, deviceState, hingeAngle?, warning? }. deviceState is the state Android committed (CLOSED, HALF_OPENED, OPENED), and posture is the matching preset. A lock screen the fold raises is dismissed; warning is set when it cannot be, or when a lock screen is showing or the screen is off after the fold. On a generic foldable AVD, closed shows only the left part of the display and describe frames do not match tap coordinates; use a Pixel Fold AVD to test the cover panel.
Fails if the device is not foldable, is a remote simulator or a physical Android device.","signature":"tools.argent.fold({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (a foldable iOS simulator UDID or Android emulator serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Where to move the hinge: `closed` shows the cover panel, `half-open` and `open` show the inner panel. */
  posture?: "closed" | "half-open" | "open",
  /** The hinge angle in degrees: 0 = closed, 180 = open. Range: 0 to 180. @minimum 0 @maximum 180 */
  angle?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["chromium-tabs"]","description":"List and switch the tabs / windows of a Chromium (CDP) app (an Electron app's BrowserWindows or a Chromium browser's tabs), and open or close them.
The active tab is what describe / gesture-tap / screenshot / debugger-evaluate / open-url all operate on, so select a tab before driving it. tabIds (`t1`, `t2`, …) are stable for the session and never reused.
Use when an app exposes multiple windows or tabs and you need to inspect or drive one other than the current page, or to open/close a page during an e2e test.
Returns { tabs: [{ tabId, targetId, title, url, active, label? }] } after the action. Fails if the device is not a Chromium (CDP) device, or the requested tabId/label no longer matches a live tab.","signature":"tools.argent["chromium-tabs"]({
  /** Chromium device id from `list-devices` (e.g. `chromium-cdp-9222`). */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * list: enumerate tabs/windows. select: make a tab active. new: open a tab and activate it. close: close a tab; if it was active, another live tab becomes active.
   */
  action: "list" | "select" | "new" | "close",
  /** Target tab for `select` (required) / `close`: a tabId like `t2` or a label. `close` defaults to the active tab. */
  tab?: string,
  /** `new` only: URL to open (defaults to about:blank). */
  url?: string,
  /** `new` only: a memorable label usable interchangeably with the tabId. */
  label?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["screenshot-diff"]","description":"Compare two PNG screenshots and return a compact visual-diff summary.
Each side is a saved PNG or a live capture from the device. baselinePath + captureCurrent is the common visual-regression flow.
Use when stable before/after screenshots exist and the expected result is pixel-visible: layout, spacing, color, typography, image/icon rendering, clipping, overflow, or text rendering.
Physical iPhones: live captures are device-wide and need no registered app. Keep baselines per device model; different aspect ratios fail as a dimension mismatch.
Mac apps: save the baseline with screenshot at scale 1.0; a resized window fails as a dimension mismatch.
Returns { summary, diffPath, contextDiffPath }. The summary uses normalized [0,1] screen locations matching describe coordinates; diffPath is the full-size diff image and contextDiffPath is a downscaled image for MCP/agent display.
Ignores the fixed top status-bar band for both pixel and OCR text comparisons.
Fails if the input sources are invalid, PNG files cannot be read, outputDir cannot be written, or the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable.","signature":"tools.argent["screenshot-diff"]({
  /** Path to the saved baseline PNG file. Required unless captureBaseline is true; do not pass both. @minLength 1 */
  baselinePath?: string,
  /** Path to the saved current PNG file. Required unless captureCurrent is true; do not pass both. @minLength 1 */
  currentPath?: string,
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial, or a Mac app id). @minLength 1 */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Capture the baseline screenshot live from the device, at full resolution, before diffing. Cannot be combined with captureCurrent.
   */
  captureBaseline?: boolean,
  /**
   * Capture the current screenshot live from the device, at full resolution, before diffing. Cannot be combined with captureBaseline.
   */
  captureCurrent?: boolean,
  /** Orientation override for live baseline/current captures. Ignored on physical iPhones. */
  rotation?: "Portrait" | "LandscapeLeft" | "LandscapeRight" | "PortraitUpsideDown",
  /**
   * Directory where diff artifacts should be written (default: a temp directory). The diff images are returned in the result either way.
   * @minLength 1
   */
  outputDir?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.screenshot","description":"Capture a PNG of the device screen (iOS simulator or physical device, Android emulator, Apple TV simulator, Vega, the window of a Mac app, or Chromium app). Returns { image }, the PNG file.
Use when you need a baseline image before an interaction or the screen state after a delay. Gestures, key presses and app launches already attach a screenshot to their result.
Fails if the capture backend (simulator-server, emulator, Chromium CDP) cannot be reached for this device.","signature":"tools.argent.screenshot({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Apple TV UDID, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Orientation override for the screenshot (on Chromium it rotates the captured image). On Android the capture already follows the device's rotation. Ignored on physical iPhones.
   */
  rotation?: "Portrait" | "LandscapeLeft" | "LandscapeRight" | "PortraitUpsideDown",
  /**
   * Scale factor. Defaults to ARGENT_SCREENSHOT_SCALE env var, or 0.25 if unset for iOS/Android. On a local Apple TV simulator, an Android TV or Vega, with neither set, the capture is downscaled to a 576 px long side (0.15 of a 4K capture). On Chromium the default is 1.0 (no downscale); pass <1 to opt in. Downscaling on Chromium requires the optional `sharp` dependency. On a Mac app the default is one pixel per window point, downscaled to a 768 px long side for a larger window. Range: 0.01 to 1.
   * @minimum 0.01 @maximum 1
   */
  scale?: number,
  /**
   * Set false only when capturing a full-resolution PNG (scale: 1.0) to save as a baseline/current for screenshot-diff. The file is still written, but the image bytes are not attached to the agent context. Default: true.
   * @default true
   */
  includeImageInContext?: boolean,
  /**
   * Downscaling algorithm when scale<1 on Chromium. Defaults to lanczos3 (highest quality). Ignored on every other platform.
   */
  downscaler?: "lanczos3" | "box" | "bilinear" | "nearest",
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]","description":"Press the device screen (iOS simulator or physical device, Android emulator, Mac app, or Chromium app) at a point given as fractions of the screen width and height (not pixels): a Down event followed by an Up event at the same spot. On a Mac app it is a click, and x/y are fractions of the area describe reports, not of the window.
Use when tapping a button, link, or any tappable element on the screen.
Returns { tapped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the device backend (simulator-server / emulator / Chromium CDP) is unreachable.
Before tapping, take the coordinates from `describe` on every platform (on Chromium it walks the DOM). More information in `argent-device-interact` skill","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). */
  udid: string,
  /** Horizontal position: 0 = left edge, 1 = right edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  x: number,
  /** Vertical position: 0 = top edge, 1 = bottom edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Number of taps/clicks dispatched as ONE gesture inside the OS double-tap window (2 = double-tap / double-click), which two separate tap calls cannot guarantee. On physical iOS, higher counts loop single taps, which a double-tap recognizer may pair. Default 1. Range: 1 to 10.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 10
   */
  clickCount?: number,
  /**
   * Mouse button, Mac app only: "right" is a right-click, for example to open a context menu; any other device refuses it. Default "left".
   */
  button?: "left" | "right",
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.keyboard","description":"Type text or press special keys on the device (iOS simulator, Android emulator or device, Chromium app, Mac app, Vega Virtual Device, or Apple TV / Android TV) using keyboard events.
Use when you need to enter text or trigger a named key such as enter, escape, or arrow keys. On Vega and Apple TV / Android TV, prefer the remote tools for D-pad navigation; focus a text field with `tv-remote` first (e.g. a search or login box), then type into it with keyboard.
Tap the field first: with no focus the text is dropped and the call still succeeds.
Returns { typed: string, keys: number }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if an unsupported key name is provided (rejected before anything is sent) or if the device's input backend is not reachable.
A failure is not rolled back. Un-typeable text is rejected mid-string on the iOS simulator and Chromium, leaving the characters before it in the field (Android, Vega and TV targets check the whole string up front). A Mac app types any character. A transport failure partway also leaves the text already sent. On a retry, read the field's actual contents; do not assume it is unchanged.
React Native: after typing, the next tap outside the field may only close the keyboard. If only the keyboard closed, tap again; where the field submits on return, `key: "enter"` avoids the extra tap.","signature":"tools.argent.keyboard({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Text to type character by character; handles uppercase, digits and common punctuation. Cannot be combined with `key`: to type and then press a key, send two `keyboard` steps in one `run-sequence` ({ text: "hello" } then { key: "enter" }). To type a credential without its plaintext ever entering your context, use a secret placeholder `{{secret:<NAME>}}`, e.g. "{{secret:APP_PASSWORD}}", alone or inside longer text. It is resolved on the machine running the tool-server, from the first source that defines the name: the `ARGENT_SECRET_<NAME>` environment variable, `.argent/secrets.env` in the project, the project's `.env.local` / `.env` (only their `ARGENT_SECRET_`-prefixed keys), then `~/.argent/secrets.env`. Nothing else on the host is reachable. Over a link with the `forward-secrets` flag on, the client's own sources are checked first. The result echoes the placeholder, never the value, and the auto-screenshot after typing is skipped. To submit after a secret, put both steps in ONE `run-sequence`: that keeps the skip covering the Enter, which a second bare `keyboard` call would not. If the secret is not set, the failure lists the available names and the sources it checked; ask the user to add it to one of them (a secrets file applies immediately, an env var needs a restart). NEVER ask the user to paste the secret value into the conversation.
   */
  text?: string,
  /**
   * Named key to press: enter, escape, backspace, tab, space, arrow-up, arrow-down, arrow-left, arrow-right, f1-f12. Cannot be combined with `text`; to do both, send two `keyboard` steps in one `run-sequence`. Not supported on Apple TV / Android TV; move focus with `tv-remote` (up/down/left/right) instead. Physical iOS: only `enter` and `backspace`. Mac app: also delete (forward delete), home, end, page-up, page-down, and shortcuts with modifiers joined by + (command, shift, option, control), e.g. command+a.
   */
  key?: string,
  /**
   * Delay in ms between key presses (default 50). Ignored on Android phones/tablets, Vega, Apple TV / Android TV and physical iOS, which type the whole string in one shot or at their own cadence.
   */
  delayMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["debugger-component-tree"]","description":"Fetch the current screen of a running React Native app as a compact tree of React components.
Each on-screen component is listed with its name, its text (or accessibilityLabel when it has no
text), [testID=…] and normalized (tap: x,y) centre in [0,1] (fractions of the screen, not pixels).
Full-screen transparent wrappers and implementation-detail components are pruned. Wrappers the size
of their parent are pruned too, so a missing name proves nothing: check with debugger-inspect-element.

Use when you need what describe does not show: a testID for a selector (on Android, describe omits
the testID of a view that has no label and is not tappable), or the component names and nesting
behind the screen before editing code. To find what to tap, use describe.

On an iOS simulator with a landscape UI (a rotated device, or an unfolded foldable), the tap
coordinates are on the screen's axes, which the gesture tools use.
Returns a compact text tree; call again after navigation. Fails if the Metro debugger is not connected.","signature":"tools.argent["debugger-component-tree"]({
  /**
   * Metro port. Omit it to use this device's port (8081 by default). Ignored for Chromium, whose CDP port is in device_id. Range: 1 to 65535.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 65535
   */
  port?: number,
  /** Device id from list-devices: the SAME id you passed to debugger-connect (iOS simulator UDID or Android serial). */
  device_id: string,
  /**
   * iOS simulator UDID from list-devices. Pass it when device_id is a logicalDeviceId (two or more devices share one Metro), so that the tap coordinates of a landscape UI are on the screen's axes. Give the UDID of the simulator that shows the app.
   */
  udid?: string,
  /**
   * When true, only components visible on screen are returned. Set to false to include all mounted components, including those scrolled off-screen, e.g. to understand the full page structure. Default: true.
   * @default true
   */
  onScreenOnly?: boolean,
  /**
   * Maximum total nodes to include. When exceeded, intermediate single-child wrapper chains are collapsed to preserve both root structure and leaf elements. Default: no limit.
   */
  maxNodes?: number,
  /**
   * When true, appends a summary of all filtered components: total fiber count and skip counts by component name and by filter. Default: false.
   * @default false
   */
  includeSkipped?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["native-find-views"]","description":"Search for specific UIViews in the running app by class name, accessibility identifier, accessibility label, tag or React Native nativeID; at least one of these params is required.
Use when you need to locate a specific view by its properties without dumping the entire native-full-hierarchy.
Returns { status: "ok", matches }: the matching views with their frames, properties, optional ancestors and optional children. Frames are in points. frame and center are relative to the superview, and windowFrame turns with the UI. To tap a view, divide the centre of its screenFrame by the screen size in points, which native-full-hierarchy returns as screen (maxDepth: 1 is enough), and pass the result to gesture-tap. Matches are not filtered for visibility. Before tapping, call native-view-at-point with hitTest: true at that centre, with skipClasses ["UITextEffectsWindow"]. If it does not return the match or a descendant, check a screenshot.
On a Mac app it searches the NSViews of every visible window: className is the NSView class, and identifier matches the accessibility identifier or the view identifier. SwiftUI sets a control's identifier and label on an accessibility element, not on an NSView, so an identifier or label search does not find SwiftUI controls: describe lists them with their identifiers and frames. The tap recipe above and the statuses below are iOS only.
If status is restart_required: follow the message (usually restart-app), then retry. If status is service_stale: the app is already injected, so restarting it cannot help. Restart the tool-server (`argent server stop && argent server start --detach`) and retry. If the same status comes back after that restart, stop restarting: follow the message, which names the terminal fallback. If status is connect_pending: the app is injected and still connecting. Do not restart it, wait a few seconds and retry. If status is init_failed: follow the message (re-boot the simulator) rather than retrying.
An Apple system app is rejected outright (terminal: never retry it).","signature":"tools.argent["native-find-views"]({
  /** Simulator UDID, or a Mac app id (macos-app-<bundle id>) */
  udid: string,
  /** Bundle ID of the app (on a Mac app, the bundle id in the device id) */
  bundleId: string,
  /** UIView class name to match (exact, e.g. UIButton) */
  className?: string,
  /** Accessibility identifier to match (exact) */
  identifier?: string,
  /** Accessibility label to match (exact) */
  label?: string,
  /** UIView tag integer to match. @integer */
  tag?: number,
  /** React Native nativeID prop to match (exact) */
  nativeID?: string,
  /** Include ancestor chain for each matched view (default true) */
  includeAncestors?: boolean,
  /** Include child views for each matched view (default true) */
  includeChildren?: boolean,
  /**
   * View fields to include. Defaults: pointer, className, tag, frame, windowFrame, screenFrame, bounds, hidden, alpha, opaque, clipsToBounds, userInteractionEnabled, depth, identifier, label, layerName, nativeID. Additional: center, transform, contentMode, backgroundColor, tintColor, value, firstResponder
   */
  fields?: Array<string>,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-scroll"]","description":"Send mouse-wheel events at a point to scroll content in a Chromium app or a Mac app. The anchor x/y and the deltas are fractions of the window (not pixels); x/y match gesture-tap and describe.
Use when content is below/above the fold (describe shows off-screen elements with zero height) or a list needs scrolling. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe.
Returns { scrolled: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device, or on a Mac app when nothing under the point scrolls.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-scroll"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Anchor x. The wheel events land here; put it over the element you want to scroll. Range: 0 to 1.
   * @minimum 0 @maximum 1
   */
  x: number,
  /** Anchor y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Horizontal scroll distance as a fraction of the window width. Positive reveals content to the right; negative scrolls back left.
   */
  deltaX?: number,
  /**
   * Vertical scroll distance as a fraction of the window height (0.5 = half a window). Positive scrolls down (reveals content below), like rolling a mouse wheel toward you; negative scrolls back up.
   */
  deltaY?: number,
  /**
   * Spread the scroll over this many milliseconds in wheel-event steps (default 300) so scroll handlers fire progressively. Ignored on a Mac app.
   */
  durationMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["native-view-at-point"]","description":"Inspect the deepest UIView at a raw native window point: the one drawn there, or with hitTest: true the one that would receive a touch.

Without hitTest it ignores userInteractionEnabled and answers "what is visually here?".
Use when a screenshot shows something the accessibility tree does not name (an
unlabeled icon, a decorative overlay, a custom-drawn cell) and you need the class,
identifier or nativeID of whatever draws it.

With hitTest: true it answers "what would receive the touch?". Use when a tap lands
somewhere unexpected or does nothing, to see which control UIKit would hand the
touch to: a transparent overlay swallowing it, a parent recognizer, a disabled button.

Returns { status: "ok", view }: the matched view with its class name, frames,
identifier, label and layer name, plus its ancestors and subviews as requested.
view is null when nothing is drawn at that point, or with hitTest when no touchable view sits under it.

To get x and y from a normalized describe frame, multiply its values by the
screen size in points, also when the UI is landscape.
native-full-hierarchy returns that size as screen (pass maxDepth: 1 and fields: ["className"] to keep it small). On a Mac app,
multiply them by the size of the area describe reports instead.

If status is restart_required: follow the message (usually restart-app), then retry. If status is service_stale: the app is already injected, so restarting it cannot help. Restart the tool-server (`argent server stop && argent server start --detach`) and retry. If the same status comes back after that restart, stop restarting: follow the message, which names the terminal fallback. If status is connect_pending: the app is injected and still connecting. Do not restart it, wait a few seconds and retry. If status is init_failed: follow the message (re-boot the simulator) rather than retrying.
An Apple system app is rejected outright (terminal: never retry it).","signature":"tools.argent["native-view-at-point"]({
  /** Simulator UDID, or a Mac app id (macos-app-<bundle id>) */
  udid: string,
  /** Bundle ID of the app (on a Mac app, the bundle id in the device id) */
  bundleId: string,
  /**
   * Raw X coordinate in points, on the portrait axes of the screen; on a Mac app, points from the top-left of the area describe reports. NOT normalized [0,1] tap space.
   */
  x: number,
  /** Raw Y coordinate in points, on the same axes as x. NOT normalized [0,1] tap space. */
  y: number,
  /** Include ancestor chain for the matched view (default true) */
  includeAncestors?: boolean,
  /** Include child views for the matched view (default false) */
  includeChildren?: boolean,
  /**
   * View fields to include. Defaults: pointer, className, tag, frame, windowFrame, screenFrame, bounds, hidden, alpha, opaque, clipsToBounds, userInteractionEnabled, depth, identifier, label, layerName, nativeID. Additional: center, transform, contentMode, backgroundColor, tintColor, value, firstResponder
   */
  fields?: Array<string>,
  /** Exact UIView class names whose entire subtree should be pruned */
  skipClasses?: Array<string>,
  /** Class name prefixes to prune entire subtrees */
  skipClassPrefixes?: Array<string>,
  /**
   * Maximum depth for returned child/ancestor serialization (default 150). Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  maxDepth?: number,
  /**
   * true returns the view that would receive a touch at the point: it respects userInteractionEnabled, close to UIKit hit-testing. Default false: the deepest visible view.
   */
  hitTest?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["tv-remote"]","description":"Press a TV remote / D-pad button (or a whole path of them) on a TV device: Apple TV (tvOS), Android TV (leanback), or Vega (Fire TV).
A TV is navigated with a directional remote, not touch. Use when you need to move focus or press a key on a TV, instead of gesture-tap/swipe (which do not apply on a TV).
Read the screen with `describe` before and after to see where focus landed.
Returns { pressed, count }. Fails on a target that is not a TV, and on the Apple TV simulator for rewind, fastForward, next, previous, volumeUp, volumeDown and mute (its HID stack ignores them); every other button works on all three platforms.","signature":"tools.argent["tv-remote"]({
  /** Target TV device id from `list-devices` (Apple TV, Android TV, or Vega). */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * A single TV-remote button, or a path of them run in one call. Move focus with up/down/left/right, confirm with select, go back with back/menu, exit with home; the media and volume buttons press the matching remote keys. For multi-step navigation pass an array, e.g. ["up","right","right","select"]; strongly prefer this over multiple `tv-remote` calls: the whole path runs in a single call. Max items: 64.
   */
  button: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right" | "select" | "back" | "home" | "menu" | "playPause" | "rewind" | "fastForward" | "next" | "previous" | "volumeUp" | "volumeDown" | "mute" | Array<"up" | "down" | "left" | "right" | "select" | "back" | "home" | "menu" | "playPause" | "rewind" | "fastForward" | "next" | "previous" | "volumeUp" | "volumeDown" | "mute">,
  /**
   * Repeat the whole `button` value this many times (default 1). Compact for long same-button runs, e.g. { button: "down", repeat: 12 }. Range: 1 to 50.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 50
   */
  repeat?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-pinch"]","description":"Execute a pinch-to-zoom gesture by moving two fingers toward or away from a center point to change the scale of on-screen content. Positions and distances are fractions of the screen (0.0–1.0, not pixels), as in gesture-tap.
startDistance > endDistance = pinch in (zoom out). startDistance < endDistance = pinch out (zoom in).
Typical values: startDistance 0.2, endDistance 0.6 for a zoom-in pinch at screen center.
Use when you need to zoom in or out on a map, image, or zoomable view. Returns { pinched: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable for the given device, or on a physical iPhone when the app is not drawn in portrait.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-pinch"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Center of the pinch, horizontal. */
  centerX: number,
  /** Center of the pinch, vertical. */
  centerY: number,
  /** Initial distance between the two fingers. */
  startDistance: number,
  /** Final distance between the two fingers. */
  endDistance: number,
  /**
   * Final horizontal center of the pinch. When set, the centroid drifts linearly from centerX to endCenterX over the gesture (e.g. to keep expanding fingers on-screen near an edge). Omit for a fixed center.
   */
  endCenterX?: number,
  /** Final vertical center of the pinch; drifts from centerY like endCenterX. Omit for a fixed center. */
  endCenterY?: number,
  /** Angle in degrees of the axis the fingers lie on: 0 = horizontal (default), 90 = vertical. */
  angle?: number,
  /** Total gesture duration in milliseconds (default 300) */
  durationMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]","description":"Press the left mouse button at a start point, move to an end point, and release: a desktop mouse drag in a Chromium app or a Mac app, with mouse moves interpolated at ~60fps. All positions are fractions of the window width/height (not pixels), the same space as gesture-tap and describe, except that on Chromium exactly 1.0 lands one pixel inside the window edge.
Use for slider thumbs, drag-and-drop, text selection, or draggable UI elements. Dragging never scrolls content on desktop; use gesture-scroll for lists/pages. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe. On a Mac app the same start and end point is a long press, and a drop lands only inside the app's own windows.
Returns { dragged: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /** Press x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Press y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** Release x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** Release y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total drag duration in milliseconds (default 300, 800 for a long press on a Mac app); the button stays down this long. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the drag releases with momentum; default true (a constant-speed drag). false decelerates into the release point, so an app that derives a fling from the release velocity (carousels, drag libraries) applies little to no momentum: use it when the drag must stop where it was aimed. Under ~100ms durationMs some fling survives; keep the default 300 when the fling must be fully suppressed.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["run-sequence"]","description":"Execute several device interaction steps in one call.
Use when every step is known in advance and you do not need to see the screen between steps (e.g. scrolling several times, typing then pressing enter). If a step depends on the result of an earlier one (e.g. a menu item that appears only after a tap), make separate calls instead.
Steps run in order. If a step fails (an error, a tool that is not allowed or not supported on the target, or an await-ui-element that returns success=false), the sequence stops there and later steps do NOT run. Returns { completed, total, steps } with a result or an error for each step that ran, so a stopped sequence returns partial results.
One screenshot and element tree are captured after the whole sequence, not per step: call screenshot separately only for a baseline before it or to see an intermediate step. A `{{secret:...}}` in any step skips that capture, so type a secret and submit it in one sequence.
On a TV target (Apple TV / Android TV / Vega) use tv-remote for remote presses and keyboard for text.

Allowed tools, their args (? = optional) and targets. Positions and distances are 0–1 fractions of the screen (window on Chromium), not pixels; angles are in degrees, durations in ms.
  gesture-tap: x, y, clickCount?, button? "left"|"right" (macos)  [ios/android/chromium/macos]
  gesture-swipe: fromX, fromY, toX, toY, durationMs?, momentum?  [ios/android]
  gesture-scroll: x, y, deltaX?, deltaY? (signed; positive scrolls down), durationMs?  [chromium/macos]
  gesture-drag: fromX, fromY, toX, toY, durationMs?, momentum?  [chromium/macos]
  gesture-custom: events: [{ type: "Down"|"Move"|"Up", x, y, x2?, y2?, delayMs? }], interpolate?  [ios/android]
  gesture-pinch: centerX, centerY, startDistance, endDistance, endCenterX?, endCenterY?, angle?, durationMs?  [ios/android]
  gesture-rotate: centerX, centerY, startAngle, endAngle, radius (or radiusX + radiusY), durationMs?  [ios/android]
  button: button: "home"|"back"|"power"|"volumeUp"|"volumeDown"|"appSwitch"|"actionButton"  [ios/android]
  keyboard: text? or key? (one per step; key e.g. "enter"), delayMs?  [all; Apple TV / Android TV: text only]
    text may hold {{secret:<NAME>}} placeholders, resolved server-side from ARGENT_SECRET_<NAME> or an argent secrets file (over a forward-secrets link, the client's first), so a credential never enters your context
  paste: text (device clipboard + paste shortcut; only where a user would paste, e.g. an OTP, else keyboard)  [ios sim/android emu/macos]
  rotate: orientation: "Portrait"|"LandscapeLeft"|"LandscapeRight"|"PortraitUpsideDown"  [ios sim/android]
  shake: count?  [ios sim/android emu]
  fold: posture? "closed"|"half-open"|"open", or angle? 0–180, exactly one (later steps land on the new panel, whose coordinates differ)  [foldable ios sim/android emu]
  tv-remote: button (one button, or an array run as a path in one step), repeat?  [apple tv/android tv/vega]
    buttons: up/down/left/right/select/back/home/menu/playPause; rewind/fastForward/next/previous/volumeUp/volumeDown/mute on Android TV and Vega only
  await-ui-element: condition: "exists"|"visible"|"hidden"|"text", selector: { text?, identifier?, role? }, expectedText? (for "text"), textMatch?, timeoutMs?, pollIntervalMs?  [all]","signature":"tools.argent["run-sequence"]({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id), shared across all steps.
   */
  udid: string,
  /** Ordered list of interaction steps to execute sequentially. @minItems 1 */
  steps: Array<{
      /** Tool name: one of the allowed tools listed in the description. */
      tool: string,
      /** Tool arguments (excluding udid, which is injected automatically) */
      args: {
        [key: string]: unknown,
      },
      /** Wait time in ms after this step before the next (default 100) */
      delayMs?: number,
    }>,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-swipe"]","description":"Execute a smooth swipe / drag touch gesture between two points on the device (iOS simulator or physical device, or Android emulator). All from/to positions are fractions of screen width/height (not pixels), same as gesture-tap.
Generates interpolated Move events for a natural feel (~60fps).
Swipe up (fromY > toY) to scroll content down.
Use when you need to scroll a list, dismiss a modal, drag an element, or navigate between pages. Not supported on Chromium; use gesture-scroll there instead. Not supported on a Mac app; use gesture-scroll or gesture-drag there.
Physical iOS: an edge gesture (back-swipe) needs fromX 0 exactly; durationMs sets drag speed, not time; momentum:false only rests 300ms at the end and does not damp.
Returns { swiped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the simulator-server / emulator backend is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-swipe"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Start x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Start y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** End x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** End y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total gesture duration in milliseconds (default 300); the finger stays down this long. Below ~32ms the whole travel lands in one or two frames, which the OS flings as hard as any flick. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the swipe releases with momentum; default true (a natural flinging swipe). false eases the finger into the end point so the OS applies little to no fling and the content stops where the finger lifts: use it when you need a deterministic scroll distance, e.g. in scroll-to-element loops. false needs durationMs >= 150 and is rejected below that; near 150 it can still land short or fling, so keep the default 300.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["menu-bar"]","description":"Read and choose the menu bar commands of a Mac app (File > Export..., Edit > Select All). The menu bar is outside the app window: describe does not show it, and gesture-tap cannot reach it.
Use when a command lives in the app's menus. Use keyboard instead for a shortcut you already know, and file-panel for the open or save panel that a command shows.
list returns { menuBar }, one line per item indented under its menu: the title (a menu ends with " >"), [disabled] or [checked], the shortcut in keyboard syntax, and id="..." when the item has one. choose takes path (the titles from the menu bar down, e.g. ["File", "Export..."]) or identifier, and returns { chosen } with the path; the command runs right after the reply.
Fails if no item matches (the error lists the titles at that level), if the item is disabled or is a menu, if a menu of the app is open, or if the device is not a Mac app.","signature":"tools.argent["menu-bar"]({
  /** Mac app id from `list-devices` (macos-app-<bundle id>). */
  udid: string,
  /** list: the app's menu bar, one line per item. choose: run one menu item, by path or identifier. */
  action: "list" | "choose",
  /**
   * For choose: the titles from the menu bar down to the item, as list shows them, e.g. ["File", "Export..."]. Case and "..." versus "…" do not matter. Max items: 9.
   * @minItems 1 @maxItems 9
   * Each item: @minLength 1
   */
  path?: Array<string>,
  /** For choose: the item's id="..." from list, instead of path. @minLength 1 */
  identifier?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-custom"]","description":"Send a sequence of touch events for complex gestures.
Use for: long press, drag-and-drop, custom scroll, pinch (second touch point).
For simple taps use the gesture-tap tool. For straight-line scrolling use the gesture-swipe tool. Not supported on a Mac app: use gesture-tap, gesture-drag and gesture-scroll there.
For pinch gestures use gesture-pinch. For rotation gestures use gesture-rotate.
All x/y values are fractions of the screen width/height (not pixels).
Physical iOS: a Down, any Moves, then an Up; x2/y2 adds a second finger. A plain Down then Up: same point = press-hold, other point = drag (Up delayMs = rest before the move); one Move at the Down point between them = long-press pickup (Move delayMs = hold, Up delayMs = move time). Any other train plays as timed paths, each event waiting its delayMs, and needs the app drawn in portrait.
Returns { events: number } with the total count of events dispatched. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the target device is not booted or an event type is invalid.

Example long-press at center:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.5},{"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.5,"delayMs":800}]

Example pick up a list item with a long press, then drag it up:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.6},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.6,"delayMs":800},{"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.3,"delayMs":500}]

Example smooth scroll down:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.7},
   {"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.6},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.5},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.4},
   {"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.3}]

Example pinch-to-zoom (with interpolate:10 for smoothness):
  events: [{"type":"Down","x":0.4,"y":0.5,"x2":0.6,"y2":0.5},
           {"type":"Up","x":0.2,"y":0.5,"x2":0.8,"y2":0.5}]
  interpolate: 10","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-custom"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Touch events, dispatched in order. */
  events: Array<{
      /** Touch event type */
      type: "Down" | "Move" | "Up",
      /** Touch x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      x: number,
      /** Touch y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      y: number,
      /** Second finger x, for two-finger gestures. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      x2?: number,
      /** Second finger y, for two-finger gestures. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      y2?: number,
      /** Delay before this event in milliseconds (default 16ms ≈ 60fps) */
      delayMs?: number,
    }>,
  /**
   * Number of Move events to insert between each pair of consecutive events, linearly interpolating both x,y and x2,y2 and splitting the delay evenly, for a smooth gesture. Default: no interpolation.
   */
  interpolate?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"}],"remaining":9,"next":{"offset":20}}

content.text:
{"items":[{"path":"tools.argent.describe","description":"Get the accessibility / DOM element tree for the current screen.
Use when you need element frames or labels and have no fresh tree: most interaction tools (not tv-remote or shake) already return one after each action, or name an identical earlier one by its tree id.
On iOS it reads the AXRuntime accessibility service, so it sees whatever is visible, including system dialogs and permission prompts. While a system dialog is visible it returns the dialog's elements (buttons, text); otherwise the foreground app's. On a physical iOS device it covers only the app started with launch-app; to read system dialogs there, launch-app `com.apple.springboard` first.
On Android it reads the tree through Argent's UiAutomation helper, falling back to `uiautomator dump`. On Chromium it walks the DOM over the Chrome DevTools Protocol: every visible element with its ARIA role, accessible name and bounding rect.
On Vega (Fire TV) it reads the on-device automation toolkit; `[focused]`/`[selected]` mark the D-pad cursor, which you move with `tv-remote` (Vega has no touch).
On a Mac app it reads the app's accessibility tree with web view content and React Native testIDs merged in; frames are fractions of the app window, grown to include sheets and menus past it.
On a TV target (Apple TV / Android TV: a `list-devices` entry with runtimeKind 'tv') it returns the FOCUSED element and the FOCUSABLE elements instead, since a TV UI has no tap coordinates. Move the highlight with `tv-remote`, then call describe again to confirm where focus landed.

Returns `{ description, source }`: `description` renders the tree one line per element, with its role, label/value/id, VoiceOver hint (iOS simulator), interactivity flags and frame. Frames are normalized [0,1] fractions of the screen / window width and height (not pixels); tap an element at the centre of its frame. The tree has no z-order or occlusion information: an element may be covered by an overlay (e.g. a toolbar over list rows), so check a screenshot when a tap lands unexpectedly.
Fails if the tree cannot be read, e.g. on a physical iOS device before launch-app has started an app.

For UIKit properties of a view (class name, accessibilityIdentifier), use native-find-views (iOS simulator only).
For React Native component names, or a testID describe does not list, use debugger-component-tree.","signature":"tools.argent.describe({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * App bundle ID, used as a target hint on iOS when the AX-service returns no elements and describe falls back to native-devtools inspection. If omitted, the fallback auto-detects the frontmost connected app. Ignored on every other platform, including a physical iOS device.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["await-screen-idle"]","description":"Block until the screen has rendered content and stopped changing, or the timeout elapses. It polls the same element tree as `describe`.
Use when a launch or navigation must finish rendering before a screenshot or tap.
Returns { settled, waitedMs, polls, note? }. If the screen never goes still before timeoutMs, it returns settled=false instead of failing.","signature":"tools.argent["await-screen-idle"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). @minLength 1 */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Max time to wait for the screen to settle before giving up (default 3000). Maximum: 120000. Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @maximum 120000 @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  timeoutMs?: number,
  /** How often to re-read the tree (default 200). Range: 50 to 5000. @integer @minimum 50 @maximum 5000 */
  pollIntervalMs?: number,
  /**
   * The screen must hold the same content for at least this long to count as settled (default 250). Range: 0 to 10000.
   * @integer @minimum 0 @maximum 10000
   */
  minStableMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["await-ui-element"]","description":"Block until a UI element reaches an expected state or the timeout elapses, so you don't have to poll screenshot/describe yourself. It polls the same tree as `describe`.
Use when the next step needs an element that appears or disappears asynchronously, e.g. after a tap or navigation, or before tapping something that loads late.
Returns { success: boolean, elapsed: number, note?, cause? }. If the condition does not hold by the timeout, it returns success=false, and that is not always a verdict on the condition: `cause` says which it was: `unmet` (the tree was read and the condition was false there), `unreadable` (no trustworthy read, so nothing was judged) or `cancelled`. `note` describes what was seen. Only `unmet` licenses rewriting the check.","signature":"tools.argent["await-ui-element"]({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * What to wait for. `exists`: the selector matches an element anywhere in the tree. `visible`: it matches one with a non-zero on-screen frame. `hidden`: it matches nothing, or only a zero-area element (e.g. a spinner that went away). `text`: the first visible match in reading order (topmost, then leftmost; the first match overall if none is visible) contains expectedText, or equals it with textMatch `equals`. Only that match is checked, so if a loose selector hits several elements, narrow it to the one holding the text. On a Mac app, a match in an open menu comes first, then one in an alert, sheet or popover, then one in the window.
   */
  condition: "exists" | "visible" | "hidden" | "text",
  /** Element to match. Give at least one field; every given field must match. */
  selector: {
    /** Case-insensitive substring of the element's visible label or value. @minLength 1 */
    text?: string,
    /**
     * The element's identifier (accessibilityIdentifier / resource-id / testid), matched case-insensitively as the exact identifier or the unqualified resource-id name ('submit' matches 'com.example.app:id/submit').
     * @minLength 1
     */
    identifier?: string,
    /** Case-insensitive substring of the element's role (e.g. AXButton, button, TextView). @minLength 1 */
    role?: string,
  },
  /**
   * Required for condition `text`: the string the checked match must contain or equal (see `textMatch`).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  expectedText?: string,
  /**
   * For condition `text`: how expectedText is compared. `contains` (default) is a case-insensitive substring; `equals` is a case-insensitive full-string match.
   */
  textMatch?: "contains" | "equals",
  /**
   * iOS app bundle id, passed to the describe fallback (see `describe`). Ignored on every other platform, including physical iOS.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
  /**
   * Max time to wait for the condition before giving up (default 5000). Maximum: 120000. Must be greater than 0.
   * @integer @maximum 120000 @exclusiveMinimum 0
   */
  timeoutMs?: number,
  /** How often to re-check the tree (default 400). Range: 50 to 5000. @integer @minimum 50 @maximum 5000 */
  pollIntervalMs?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.fold","description":"Move the hinge of a foldable iOS simulator (the iPhone Duo) or Android emulator (a Pixel Fold or generic foldable AVD) to a `posture` or to an `angle`; pass exactly one of the two. The tool returns when the device accepts input again, so the next tap lands.
Use when you must see or test an app in a different posture. `list-devices` marks foldable devices with `foldable: true`. To turn a device without a fold, use `rotate`.
All tools use the active panel (the panel that shows the UI), also after a fold made outside argent. The coordinates and the screenshot size change with the panel. Before you tap, read the element tree in this result or run `describe` again.
iOS: unfolded, the UI is landscape, as on a rotated iPhone. A fold between two angles that are not closed or open can keep the current panel. To change panels, fold to closed or open.
iOS returns { activeScreen, screen: { id, panel, width, height }, posture?, hingeAngle, warning? }. screen names the active panel and gives its size in pixels. posture is set only at 0°, 120° and 180°. warning is set when argent cannot find the active panel, or when the device did not change to the expected panel.
Android returns { posture?, deviceState, hingeAngle?, warning? }. deviceState is the state Android committed (CLOSED, HALF_OPENED, OPENED), and posture is the matching preset. A lock screen the fold raises is dismissed; warning is set when it cannot be, or when a lock screen is showing or the screen is off after the fold. On a generic foldable AVD, closed shows only the left part of the display and describe frames do not match tap coordinates; use a Pixel Fold AVD to test the cover panel.
Fails if the device is not foldable, is a remote simulator or a physical Android device.","signature":"tools.argent.fold({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (a foldable iOS simulator UDID or Android emulator serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Where to move the hinge: `closed` shows the cover panel, `half-open` and `open` show the inner panel. */
  posture?: "closed" | "half-open" | "open",
  /** The hinge angle in degrees: 0 = closed, 180 = open. Range: 0 to 180. @minimum 0 @maximum 180 */
  angle?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["chromium-tabs"]","description":"List and switch the tabs / windows of a Chromium (CDP) app (an Electron app's BrowserWindows or a Chromium browser's tabs), and open or close them.
The active tab is what describe / gesture-tap / screenshot / debugger-evaluate / open-url all operate on, so select a tab before driving it. tabIds (`t1`, `t2`, …) are stable for the session and never reused.
